gl: Keep the GLES2 fallback on Linux

Upstream deprecated GLES2 in M146 (crbug.com/444049511): when an OpenGL
ES 3.0 context cannot be created, the GPU process no longer retries ES 2.0
but gives up on hardware acceleration entirely, dropping to software
compositing with no WebGL (the automatic SwiftShader fallback is also
deprecated). On VideoCore IV devices (Pi 0-3), whose vc4 driver correctly
reports OpenGL ES 2.0 only, this silently removes all hardware
acceleration.

Until M150 the behaviour was governed by kFallbackToSWIfGLES3NotSupported,
which we disabled by default. In M151 upstream removed the feature on
Linux altogether -- it is now declared only for Windows and ChromeOS, and
ShouldFallbackToSWIfGLES3NotSupported() returns a hard-coded true
everywhere else, with no flag left to override it. So return false in that
branch instead, restoring the pre-M146 ES 2.0 fallback.

The result is only consulted when ES3 context creation fails (it feeds
GLContextAttribs::allow_es_version_fallback), so ES3-capable devices (v3d
on Pi 4/5) are unaffected. Google carved ChromeOS Flex out of the same
feature for the same reason (GLES2-only hardware in the field; see
ShouldFallbackToSWIfGLES3NotSupported in this file).
--- a/ui/gl/gl_features.cc
+++ b/ui/gl/gl_features.cc
@@ -277,7 +277,11 @@ bool ShouldFallbackToSWIfGLES3NotSupport
 #elif BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)
   return base::FeatureList::IsEnabled(kFallbackToSWIfGLES3NotSupported);
 #else   // BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
-  return true;
+  // Raspberry Pi: keep the ES 2.0 fallback. On GLES2-only VideoCore IV
+  // devices (Pi 0-3) returning true replaces hardware ES 2.0 with software
+  // rendering. Only consulted when ES3 context creation fails, so
+  // ES3-capable hardware (v3d on Pi 4/5) is unaffected.
+  return false;
 #endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
 }
 
